<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Aboh</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Aboh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Igbo river kingdom near the head of the Niger Delta whose canoe fleets controlled trade on the lower Niger in the era of palm oil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aboh was founded, according to tradition, in the 17th or early 18th century by migrants from the area of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, who settled at the point where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> divides into the creeks of the delta. Ruled by an Obi in a style influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, it grew into the strongest power on the lower river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Obi ruled with titled chiefs, and the kingdom commanded fleets of large war and trading canoes. Aboh was a middleman between the hinterland and the coastal states such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a>, carrying slaves and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> downstream. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obi-ossai">Obi Ossai</a> received the explorers Richard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lander">John Lander</a> in 1830 and negotiated with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Expedition of 1841.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British and company steamers in the late 19th century broke the river middlemen, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> dominated trade. Aboh was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a> by 1900, and the Obi of Aboh continues as a traditional ruler in Delta State.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-aboh.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Aboh" width="320" height="463"><figcaption>Image: Kingsley Oladipo Ogedengbe · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aboh_at_its_territorial_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aboh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Obi Ossai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Igbo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Igbo (Ukwuani)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aboh, Abo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=kingdom-of-aboh" aria-label="Kingdom of Aboh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4861 1970 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Aboh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5181 2226L5187 2202"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5187 2202h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5181 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5181 2226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aboh. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=kingdom-of-aboh">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

